1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,704
Not every enquiry is complicated. Sometimes a guest just says what
they want.

2
00:00:05,784 --> 00:00:13,312
Anna writes four lines: two of them, three nights from October
seventeenth, and a question about diving.

3
00:00:13,392 --> 00:00:17,560
That is everything a booking needs — and nothing is written in a
form.

4
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:19,432
Watch what comes back.

5
00:00:19,512 --> 00:00:30,352
Every fact Anna gave is already recorded — her name, the date, the
nights, both guests. Each one carries the exact words she used as
its evidence.

6
00:00:30,432 --> 00:00:36,232
One thing is genuinely open: she mentioned diving but never said
which days.

7
00:00:36,312 --> 00:00:39,352
So the assistant asks that, and only that.

8
00:00:39,432 --> 00:00:45,544
Anna answers in one message: one room, diving on the eighteenth and
nineteenth.

9
00:00:45,624 --> 00:00:53,176
The assistant fills in the remaining fields — and note the transfer,
which it works out from her asking about airport pickup.

10
00:00:53,256 --> 00:00:56,848
Every field a quote depends on is now complete.

11
00:00:56,928 --> 00:01:01,672
The assistant reads the whole booking back, so Anna can check it
herself.

12
00:01:01,752 --> 00:01:08,008
And notice what it does not do. It quotes no price. It confirms
nothing as booked.

13
00:01:08,088 --> 00:01:12,472
It says plainly that someone from the team will follow up.

14
00:01:12,552 --> 00:01:22,456
Meals and the transfer type are marked as assumptions, because the
guest never chose them — a house norm did, and a guest should be
able to correct that.
